summary_en.reading_guide:
Read it as a training-signal paper, not as a new RL objective.

A good skim order is:
1. the motivation for group filtering and why skewed pass rates are still weak under GRPO/RLOO;
2. the section deriving why 50% is the most informative regime;
3. the Prefix Sampling pipeline and bucket design;
4. the main efficiency tables and any plots showing pass-rate redistribution over training.

If you only have 20–30 minutes, focus on the method figure, the derivation of the 50% target, and the main efficiency results. The main question to keep in mind is whether the speedup truly comes from better contrastive signal under fixed rollout cost, rather than hidden extra compute or replay-induced bias.

For pretraining-oriented readers, the useful takeaway is broader: when supervision is sparse and sample generation is expensive, controlling where training examples land in signal space can matter more than increasing raw sample count.

summary_en.experiments:
From the provided full-text content, the experimental setup spans multiple base models, two critic-free RL algorithms, and several reward designs. The headline result is consistent across these settings: pass-rate rewards do not reliably outperform binary rewards in final code-generation performance.

The most important empirical observations are about training dynamics rather than a single benchmark number:
- pass-rate rewards increase the fraction of non-zero-reward rollouts;
- this extra density does not translate into better pass-all-tests outcomes;
- on hard problems where no rollout is fully correct, partial-pass samples produce noisy and weak policy updates;
- some high-pass-rate programs are shortcut solutions rather than genuinely correct ones;
- reweighted or two-stage variants may help optimization in limited regimes, but they do not overturn the main conclusion.

The excerpt provided here does not include the full numeric tables, model sizes, or exact benchmark scores, so those should be checked directly in the main results tables and appendix rather than inferred.

summary_en.reading_guide:
must_read
- Key sections: Introduction; Physics Evaluator (formal definitions of 4 aspects/9 constraints, differentiable vs non-differentiable split, correlation with human judgments); Scene-GRPO (how evaluator scores become preference signals and how group-relative advantages are formed); TTO (objective, gradients, convergence/speed); Experiments + ablations (remove GRPO/TTO/constraint groups).
- Key figures/tables: qualitative failure-case comparisons (collision/floating/instability/navigability), per-constraint breakdown (tables or radar plots), human-alignment plots, main physics-score improvement table, and visual-fidelity metrics to confirm no regression.
- Innovation checklist: (i) stability of the evaluator→preference-training loop and its effect on diversity; (ii) effectiveness/limits of injecting non-differentiable constraints via preference learning; (iii) whether TTO introduces local minima or harms visual consistency and how trade-offs are managed.

skim
- Read the 4-aspect/9-constraint evaluator list and motivation;
- Skim the overall PhyMix diagram to understand GRPO (train) vs TTO (test);
- Jump to main physics-score tables and qualitative examples;
- Glance at ablations to see which constraints/stages drive gains.
